The '''2026 Newcastle-under-Lyme Borough Council election''' was held on Thursday 7 May 2026 to elect all 44 councillors who serve on [[Newcastle-under-Lyme Borough Council]]. It was held [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|alongside council elections across England]].

[[Reform UK]] won 27 of the seats, an overall majority, gaining control of the council from the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]]. The Conservatives won 15 seats and [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] won 2 seats.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Price |first1=Richard |last2=Aitken |first2=Jennie |title=Reform gains control of Newcastle-under-Lyme in historic night for party |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cwy2e2w7y46o |access-date=8 May 2026 |work=BBC News |date=8 May 2026}}</ref>

== Background ==
Simon Tagg had served as Leader of Newcastle-under-Lyme Borough Council since December 2017. In the [[2025 Staffordshire County Council election]], Newcastle-under-Lyme experienced a significant political shift towards [[Reform UK|Reform]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrews |first=Rob |date=2025-05-02 |title=Live: Reform UK win eight out of nine council seats across Newcastle |url=https://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/news/stoke-on-trent-news/live-updates-keele-nine-seats-10149986 |access-date=2025-11-13 |website=Stoke on Trent Live |language=en}}</ref> which secured eight of the nine available seats in the area and 44.7% of the total vote. [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] failed to win any seats, while the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] retained only one.

Reform gained its first seat on the council earlier in May 2025 through a by-election victory in [[Knutton]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sutton |first=Jonathan |date=2025-05-02 |title=Reform UK double delight as Lynn Dean wins May Bank & Wolstanton |url=https://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/news/stoke-on-trent-news/reform-uk-double-delight-lynn-10146234 |access-date=2025-11-13 |website=Stoke-on-Trent Live |language=en}}</ref> Lynn Dean, the elected councillor for Knutton, was suspended from Reform UK in March 2026 following allegations of racist comments made on social media.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Price |first=Richard |date=2026-03-27 |title=Reform councillor suspended following racism row |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5y7ezxqx04o |access-date=2026-03-27 |website=BBC News |language=en}}</ref> Dean subsequently stepped down from her local council role but continues to serve as a County Councillor.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Price |first=Richard |date=2026-04-01 |title=Ex-Reform councillor steps down after racism row |url=//www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9d4q4qzy7vo |access-date=2026-04-01 |website=BBC News |language=en}}</ref>

Following his defection from the Conservative Party in December 2025,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Flash |first=Oprah |date=2025-12-01 |title=Ex-deputy chair among Tory defections to Reform |url=//www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cgr12e7259xo |access-date=2026-04-01 |website=BBC News |language=en}}</ref> [[Jonathan Gullis]], the former [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|MP]] for [[Stoke-on-Trent North]], announced his candidacy for the Kidsgrove and Ravenscliffe ward representing Reform UK.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Corrigan |first=Phil |date=2026-04-01 |title=Ex-MP Jonathan Gullis to stand in election for Reform UK |url=//www.stokesentinel.co.uk/news/stoke-on-trent-news/ex-mp-jonathan-gullis-stand-10895645 |access-date=2026-04-01 |website=Stoke on Trent Live |language=en}}</ref>
Additionally, the former MP for [[Stafford (constituency)|Stafford]], [[Jeremy Lefroy]] announced he was standing for candidacy for the Maer and Whitmore ward representing the Conservatives. Both were elected.

A major topic of local concern was the [[2024–present structural changes to local government in England|proposed reorganisation of local government]], with widespread opposition to the government's plans to potentially merge [[Staffordshire Moorlands District Council|Staffordshire Moorlands]], [[Stoke-on-Trent City Council|Stoke-on-Trent]], and Newcastle-under-Lyme councils, or alternatively divide Staffordshire along east–west lines.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-08 |title=Five different plans for re-drawing Staffordshire's political map |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c4gplen51klo |access-date=2025-11-13 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> A large number of residents had signed a petition protesting these proposals.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrews |first=Rob |date=2025-01-20 |title='No to merger with Stoke' protest in Newcastle borough independence fight |url=https://www.stokesentinel.co.uk/news/stoke-on-trent-news/no-merger-stoke-protest-newcastle-9877564 |access-date=2025-11-13 |website=Stoke-on-Trent Live |language=en}}</ref>

